      "n": 924,
      "kind": "merged",
      "draft": false,
      "head": "fa4046a6b4bab0664e7f6504cb1a7efcd87c298f",
      "title": "prove: mm ktests with strengthened VmReader/VmWriter contracts",
      "body": "## Specs and properties supplemented by the ktest verification\n\n### 1. New proof function in the specs layer (`ostd/specs/mm/io.rs`)\n\n| Addition | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `VmIoOwner::tracked_shrink_range_front(nbytes)` | Shrinks the owner's range to its front `nbytes` bytes, preserving `id` / `is_kernel` / `is_fallible` / `mem_view` plus the three view-predicate implications — so the caller's `wf` relation survives a `limit` of the window (both limit tests rely on it) |\n\n### 2. Contract additions in `mm::io` (`ostd/src/mm/io.rs`)\n\n| API | Properties added |\n|---|---|\n| `VmWriter::from` / `VmReader::from` (reshaped from the `From` trait during the migration) | Full owner out-param contracts: `inv`, `wf(owner)`, view kind (write_view / read_view_initialized), `cursor.range == owner.range`, `owner.range.len == slice.len()`, kernel bounds (gated with `len == 0 \\|\\|` for the empty-slice case) |\n| Infallible `write_val` | `avail >= size_of::<T>() ==> r is Ok` (kernel-space writes cannot fault); `avail < size_of::<T>() ==> r == Err(InvalidArgs)`; `final(owner).has_write_view()` (unconditional); the Err branch pins both `*self` and `*owner` |\n| Infallible `read_val` | Same family: bidirectional Ok/Err conditions, `final(owner).read_view_initialized()`, Err branch pins both `*self` and `*owner` |\n| Infallible `read_once` | Short-read `==> Err(InvalidArgs)` row plus Err-branch double pinning |\n| Fallible `write_val` / `read_val` | Sufficient condition for the shortfall direction: `==> r == Err(InvalidArgs)` |\n| `has_remain` / `has_avail` | `returns remain_spec() > 0` / `avail_spec() > 0` (exact boolean) |\n| `limit` (reader and writer) | Exact value `== (if old <= max { old } else { max })` |\n| `to_fallible` (reader and writer) | Symmetric `requires self.inv()` / `ensures r.inv()` |\n\n### 3. Rustdoc / contract alignment\n\n`read_val`'s Verified Properties section now states what the contract actually\ndelivers instead of promising a nonexistent `val_owner` — the documented root\ncause of why the original value assertions could not be restored.\n\n",
